course: dictionary
lesson: 10219
title: Quiet laughs and tittering
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To titter is to laugh quietly, often trying not to be heard. It is a nervous, silly or slightly rude sort of laugh. «I don't know the meaning of half those long words, and, what's more, I don't believe you do either!», and some birds titter (giggle). The brackets tell you titter means giggle, a small laugh.
explain: In a classroom, a titter often goes round the room when something embarrassing happens. People try to hold it in, but it still leaks out. Ask yourself: was the laugh loud and open, or quiet and hidden? If it was quiet and hidden, it was probably a titter.
explain: Titter can also be a noun. A titter is such a little laugh. The verb forms titters, tittered and tittering show the laughing happening: the children tittered, or the room is tittering.
fact: Titter is a verb and a noun: you can titter, or you can give a titter.
fact: «Titter» means to laugh quietly in a nervous, silly or slightly rude way.
fact: In stories, people may titter behind their hands, sometimes unkindly.
fact: Similar words are giggle, snigger and chuckle. They are all kinds of laugh, but each one has its own feel.
fact: When you meet a new word, check the words around it and the brackets, if there are any, for clues.
exercise: «...and some birds titter (giggle).» What does titter mean here? | To fly away fast | To sing loudly | *To laugh quietly | To sleep in a tree
exercise: Which word means the same as titter? | shout | yawn | *giggle | roar
exercise: In the story, people tittered behind their hands. What does that tell you? | They were clapping loudly | They were fast asleep | They were crying in the rain | *They were laughing quietly, and perhaps unkindly
test: Which sentence uses titter as a noun? | He will titter tomorrow. | The class started to titter. | *She gave a little titter at the joke.
test: Why might people titter in class when something embarrassing happens? | *They find it funny but feel nervous about laughing out loud | They are sad and want to leave | They are angry and want to shout | They are tired and want to sleep
exam: Ella tittered behind her hand when the speaker stumbled. How did she laugh? | Angrily and sadly | *Quietly, trying not to be seen | Slowly and sleepily | Loudly and proudly
